{"word": "abortion", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "abortion", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/əˈbɔɹ.ʃn̩/", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "abortions", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(medicine) The expulsion from the womb of a foetus or embryo before it is fully developed, with loss of the foetus.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "(now uncommon in general use) A spontaneous abortion; a miscarriage.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Swines-bread, so used, doth not onely speed / A tardy labour; but (without great heed) / If over it a Child-great Woman stride, / Instant abortion often doth betide.", "reference": "1605 [1578], Josuah, transl. Sylvester, “The Third Day of the First Week”, in Devine Weekes and Workes, translation of La Premiere Sepmaine by Guillaume de Salluste Du Bartas, lines 693–696:"}, {"text": "At any time after impregnation, abortion may take place: it is one of the most common complaints of pregnancy, whence it is a matter of no small consequence that every practitioner should well understand it.", "reference": "1809, William Nicholson, The British Encyclopaedia, volume IV:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "An induced abortion.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["Mary decided to have an abortion because she was too young to raise a baby."], "quotes": [{"text": "It is impossible for an abortion clinic to have a waiting list of more than nine months.", "reference": "1997, George Carlin, Brain Droppings, New York: Hyperion Books, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, →OL, page 93:"}, {"text": "The story of Ms. He and her mother began in the early 1960s, shortly before the Cultural Revolution shook China. Her young parents, who